            <ArticleTitle>Dietary tocopherols inhibit PhIP-induced prostate carcinogenesis in CYP1A-humanized mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>71-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.canlet.2015.11.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0304-3835(15)00684-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Tocopherols, the major forms of vitamin E, exist as alpha-tocopherol (a-T), ß-T, ?-T and d-T. The cancer preventive activity of vitamin E is suggested by epidemiological studies, but recent large-scale cancer prevention trials with high dose of a-T yielded disappointing results. Our hypothesis that other forms of tocopherols have higher cancer preventive activities than a-T was tested, herein, in a novel prostate carcinogenesis model induced by 2-amino-1-methyl-6-phenylimidazo [4,5-b] pyridine (PhIP), a dietary carcinogen, in the CYP1A-humanized (hCYP1A) mice. Treatment of hCYP1A mice with PhIP (200?mg/kg b.w., i.g.) induced high percentages of mouse prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia (mPIN), mainly in the dorsolateral glands. Supplementation with a ?-T-rich mixture of tocopherols (?-TmT, 0.3% in diet) significantly inhibited the development of mPIN lesions and reduced PhIP-induced elevation of 8-oxo-deoxyguanosine, COX-2, nitrotyrosine, Ki-67 and p-AKT, and the loss of PTEN and Nrf2. Further studies with purified d-T, ?-T or a-T (0.2% in diet) showed that d-T was more effective than ?-T or a-T in preventing mPIN formations and p-AKT elevation. These results indicate that ?-TmT and d-T could be effective preventive agents of prostate cancer.</AbstractText>
